      "Once the **prothrombinase** (Xa + Va + Ca²⁺ + PL3) is formed, it converts **prothrombin to thrombin**. **Thrombin** then turns fibrinogen into fibrin in three phases: (1) **proteolytic** — thrombin + Ca²⁺ cleave fibrinogen into **fibrin monomers**; (2) **polymerization** — the monomers polymerize spontaneously through hydrogen bonds (an unstable clot); (3) **stabilization** — **factor XIIIa** replaces the hydrogen bonds with **covalent bonds** → a chemically stable fibrin clot (plasma, RBCs, platelets), adherent to the wall. **Clot retraction**: 20-60 min after onset, it requires **intact platelets** and normal fibrinogen (2-4 g/L); **thrombosthenin** contracts the projections of platelets bound to the fibrin network → shortening of the filaments, expulsion of serum and cells → a chemically and mechanically stable clot. The clot is then invaded by fibroblasts (repair in 1-2 weeks), then lysed (fibrinolysis), making the vessel patent again in 2-3 days.",
